  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 335 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nA strong jet will settle across the Pacific Northwest in the upper \nlevels through the mid to late week. Height rises will build in \nacross the Northern Plains bringing calmer weather to the region. \nDaytime highs will climb in to mid 60s to near 70s across western \nand north central Nebraska, Wednesday through most of the weekend. \nThis will be about 5 to 10F degrees above climatological norms \nfor this time of the year. By the end of the week, a closed low \nwill make landfall in the Pacific Northwest/Canada border, \ncreating deep troughing southward of it across the West Coast. \nThis will be the next system to keep an eye of as it should lead\nto more southwesterly flow aloft in the Plains region and \nforecasted to have multiple shortwaves crossing the Rockies over\nthe next week or so. As such, models have started to catch on \nto the potential for increased precipitation chances for our \nregion heading into the weekend and early next week. As it \nstands now, the early weekend precipitation potential looks to \nremain isolated in nature across southwest to central Nebraska, \nwith limited accumulations expected. The late weekend to early \nnext week disturbance brings a better potential for meaningful \nprecipitation potential in way of mostly rain, wintery mix \npossible in the overnights. This is with the added caveat of \ngreater uncertainty even with both the Euro and GFS both \nagreeing on overall coverage but differing on intensity and \nprecipitation type. With details still being murky, this will be\nsomething to keep an eye on with subsequent forecast details.",
